    "a": "Scope it in units, not hours: masters, assets, variants and adaptations, with volumes and specifications. Add sections on rights, AI tool use and oversight, trained models and training data, data security, labeling per market and an all-in cost breakdown. Publish the evaluation criteria and their weights in the RFP, before any bid arrives."
  },
  {
    "q": "What units should an AI production statement of work use?",
    "a": "Units that describe accepted results: master, asset, variant, adaptation, revision round, change request, usable second and acceptance. No trade body publishes these definitions, so write them into the statement of work. The 2025 US commercials agreement offers one published boundary: a changed concept or message makes a new commercial."
  },
  {
    "q": "How should procurement score AI production vendors?",
    "a": "State every criterion and its relative importance before bids arrive, and say how price relates to the rest. US federal rules and UK government guidance both require that. Neither publishes a price-to-quality split for creative services, so set your own weights, test them, and be wary of scoring price against the lowest bid."
  },
  {
    "q": "Which contract clauses matter for AI-generated content?",
    "a": "Output ownership, ownership and handover of any trained model, a ban on training general models with your material without consent, a third-party infringement guarantee, talent consent, labeling by market, data security by standard, and exit terms covering files, provenance and deletion. This is procurement practice, not legal advice."
  },
  {
    "q": "What does AI content production cost?",
