  <h2>Questions procurement asks about AI content production</h2>

  <h3>How do I write an RFP for AI content production?</h3>

  <p>Scope it in units, not hours: masters, assets, variants and adaptations, with volumes and specifications. Add sections on rights, AI tool use and oversight, trained models and training data, data security, labeling per market and an all-in cost breakdown. Publish the evaluation criteria and their weights in the RFP, before any bid arrives.</p>

  <h3>What units should an AI production statement of work use?</h3>

  <p>Units that describe accepted results: master, asset, variant, adaptation, revision round, change request, usable second and acceptance. No trade body publishes these definitions, so write them into the statement of work. The 2025 US commercials agreement offers one published boundary: a changed concept or message makes a new commercial.</p>

  <h3>How should procurement score AI production vendors?</h3>

  <p>State every criterion and its relative importance before bids arrive, and say how price relates to the rest. US federal rules and UK government guidance both require that. Neither publishes a price-to-quality split for creative services, so set your own weights, test them, and be wary of scoring price against the lowest bid.</p>

  <h3>Which contract clauses matter for AI-generated content?</h3>

  <p>Output ownership, ownership and handover of any trained model, a ban on training general models with your material without consent, a third-party infringement guarantee, talent consent, labeling by market, data security by standard, and exit terms covering files, provenance and deletion. This is procurement practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h3>What does AI content production cost?</h3>

  <p>Published category bands in September 2026: managed design subscriptions from roughly $6,000 to $15,000 a month, unlimited-request tiers from roughly $700 to $4,500, and standard ecommerce capture at $39 to $50 an image. Generated video lists at roughly $0.05 to $0.60 a second, billed per attempt. Real numbers come from bids in your units.</p>

  <h3>Who owns models trained during an engagement?</h3>

  <p>Whoever the contract says. No instrument read assigns a trained model by default, and copyright does not settle it for prompt-driven output. US federal buyers are told to secure rights to models produced in performance of a contract, with portability. A private buyer can write the same terms into its own agreement.</p>
